content:
maria: Heads-up — found a leak in the Go generator onboarding sample. Example uses context.Background in the demo which spawns a streaming goroutine that never exits on ctrl-c / shutdown. Repro + minimal fix below.

alex: ugh. can you paste the minimal repro?

maria: sure — simplified main from onboarding repo:
```go
func main() {
    // BAD: uses background so cancel from caller doesn't propagate
    ctx := context.Background()
    gen := redwood.NewGenerator(nil)
    stream, _ := gen.StreamGenerate(ctx, "tell me a joke")
    go func() {
        for tok := range stream.Chan() {
            fmt.Print(tok)
        }
    }()
    // process runs forever unless process killed, goroutine leaks on app shutdown
}
```

samir: I can repro this locally within the tutorial container. the pattern is subtle because most examples omit signal handling.

maria: fix is to accept a ctx from main and pass it down, and to stop the reader when ctx.Done() triggers. suggested change:
```go
func main() {
    ctx, cancel := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt)
    defer cancel()
    gen := redwood.NewGenerator(nil)
    stream, err := gen.StreamGenerate(ctx, "tell me a joke")
    if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
    for {
        select {
        case <-ctx.Done():
            // ensure stream is closed / handshake torn down cleanly
            stream.Close()
            return
        case tok, ok := <-stream.Chan():
            if !ok { return }
            fmt.Print(tok)
        }
    }
}
```

jen: can we standardize this pattern across all Go sample apps? Onboarding users copy-paste these examples and the leak is a poor first impression.

maria: yep — I'll open a PR to update quickstart, generator samples, and add a tiny smoketest that runs the sample and sends SIGINT to assert no goroutines remain.

samir: one more wrinkle — I noticed auth refresh during streaming sometimes restarts the generator handshake which leaves a stray goroutine reading a stale channel. it shows up in logs as repeated handshake messages and duplicate timeouts.

alex: hmm — that sounds like a retry-on-auth path that doesn't tear down the previous stream properly. we do token refresh in background via the http client, so generator reconnect needs to be able to cancel old readers.

maria: proposal: 1) make the stream object hold an internal ctx that we cancel before attempting a reconnect, 2) ensure StreamGenerate returns a stream wrapper that is safe for repeated handshake attempts, and 3) document auth retry semantics in the generator README.

jen: agree. also add a short note in the migration guide: "Do not use context.Background() in examples — prefer signal.NotifyContext or pass ctx from callers."

dev-bot: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/onboarding/pull/4123 — updates quickstart generator examples and adds SIGINT smoketest :white_check_mark:

maria: PR #4123 includes the test and example changes. tagging @alex @samir for a quick review. i'll also add a tiny section to the Go SDK README showing the recommended stream shutdown pattern and a code snippet we can copy into docs.

alex: will review today. thanks for catching this — small DX fixes like this save a ton of support time. :+1:

samir: thanks — i'll add a CI job that runs the smoketest in a short container and fails if goroutines leak.

jen: can someone add a short blurb to release notes? onboarding UX change, low risk.

maria: done — release note draft in the PR description.

alex: after merge, let's also scan other languages for similar onboarding patterns (python, node) and mirror the pattern guidance.

maria: sounds good. closing the loop here — will follow up with the PR link and changelog entry once merged. :rocket:
